title: The Economic Crisis and the Base Reforms
new-page: true
is-card: true
view-if: crisis_urgency >= 1 and crisis_program_timer == 0 and reformas_de_base == 0 and goulart_crisis_seen == 1
frequency: 200
on-arrival: month_actions += 1; crisis_program_timer = 5
card-image: img/greve.jpg
tags: party_affairs, govt_affairs

= Addressing the Economic Crisis

Brazil's economy is in turmoil. Inflation devours the wages of workers and peasants alike, foreign debt grows, and the promises of industrialization ring hollow for millions in the favelas and the drought-stricken Northeast.

Our party is divided on how to respond.

The *moderate* tendency, influenced by figures like Prestes and the old PCB tradition, argues that the immediate task is to support Goulart's nationalist government and pressure it from the left — a democratic, anti-imperialist front before any socialist transformation.

The economist and party intellectual Caio Prado Júnior has long argued for a different analysis: that Brazil's fundamental problem is not feudalism but capitalism itself, and that the Peasant Leagues and the labor movement must press for radical structural reforms — agrarian reform, nationalization of key industries, a break with the IMF — not merely tactical support for a bourgeois president.

His opponents argue that such demands will isolate the party and provoke a coup before the conditions for revolution are ripe.

Meanwhile, the *armed struggle* tendency, led by figures like Grabois and Amazonas, argues that all of this is moot — the right will never permit reforms through legal means, and the party must begin preparing the material conditions for armed resistance.

And the *reformist* tendency argues for a more limited program: targeted price controls, a modest land statute, and wage indexation — measures achievable within the existing constitutional framework without provoking a violent reaction.

- @support_moderate: Support the moderate democratic-front line.
- @support_structural: Support Caio Prado's structural reform line.
- @support_armed: Support the armed struggle tendency.
- @support_reformist: Support the limited reformist program.
- @defer: Defer making a decision until we have more information.

@support_moderate
subtitle: Build the broadest possible democratic front around Goulart.
on-arrival: moderate_strength += 5; moderate_dissent -= 5; combative_dissent += 10; structural_dissent += 8; reformas_support -= 1

The moderate line holds that the immediate enemy is not the national bourgeoisie but imperialism and the latifundia. Goulart's government, however imperfect, is a shield behind which the popular movement can grow. Pushing too hard for socialist transformation risks handing the right the pretext it needs for a coup.

Grabois and the combative tendency are scathing in their criticism — this is the same reformism, they say, that has kept the Brazilian left permanently subordinated to bourgeois politics. But the moderate line has deep roots in the party.

Our current line is one of critical support for the Goulart government and pressure for gradual reform.

@support_structural
subtitle: Press for radical structural reforms — land, nationalization, a break with the IMF.
unavailable-subtitle: The structural reform tendency is not strong enough.
choose-if: historical_mode == 0 or (structural_strength >= combative_strength and structural_strength >= moderate_strength and structural_strength >= reformist_strength)
on-arrival: moderate_dissent += 10; reformist_dissent += 6; structural_strength += 5; reformas_support += 1
go-to: reformas_adopted if reformas_support >= 3; reformas_progress if reformas_support < 3

@reformas_progress

We press for a program of genuine structural transformation: agrarian reform without compensation to the latifundistas, nationalization of oil, minerals, and banking, and a repudiation of IMF conditionality. Caio Prado's analysis gives our demands theoretical coherence — this is not adventurism, but a sober reckoning with the actual class structure of Brazilian society.

The moderates accuse us of playing into the hands of the coup plotters. The armed tendency thinks we are still too timid. We have not yet convinced the party to formally adopt the program. More work needs to be done.

@reformas_adopted
on-arrival: reformas_de_base = 1; structural_dissent -= 15; reformist_dissent -= 8; structural_strength += 10; moderate_strength -= 10; moderate_dissent += 20; combative_dissent += 5; coup_progress += 1; crisis_program_timer = 0

With sustained effort, we have convinced the party to formally commit to a program of radical structural reforms — the *Reformas de Base* in their fullest sense. [? if pcdo_in_coalition == 1: We can begin pressing Goulart to implement the reforms immediately, though resistance from conservative ministers will be fierce. ?][? if pcdo_in_coalition == 0: We must make the Reformas de Base the centerpiece of our agitation and electoral propaganda. ?]

The right will not sit still. The CIA station in Rio has already taken notice.

@support_armed
subtitle: Begin preparing the conditions for armed resistance.
unavailable-subtitle: The armed struggle tendency is not strong enough.
choose-if: historical_mode == 0 or (combative_strength >= structural_strength and combative_strength >= moderate_strength and combative_strength >= reformist_strength)
on-arrival: combative_strength += 10; reformist_dissent += 5; moderate_dissent += 8; armed_struggle_line += 2
go-to: armed_progress if armed_struggle_line < 4; armed_adopted if armed_struggle_line >= 4

@armed_progress

We argue that the Brazilian ruling class will never permit meaningful reform through legal channels — the record of the last twenty years proves it. The party must begin serious preparation: building clandestine cells, establishing rural bases, making contact with sympathetic officers, and developing the organizational capacity for a sustained campaign of resistance.

We have not yet won the party to a fully articulated armed line. The debate continues. More work needs to be done.

@armed_adopted
on-arrival: combative_strength += 15; combative_dissent -= 10; reformist_dissent += 12; moderate_dissent += 10; armed_struggle_adopted = 1; clandestine_network += 2; coup_progress += 2; crisis_program_timer = 0; reformas_de_base = 3

The party has committed to preparing the conditions for armed struggle. This is not adventurism — it is a sober recognition that the Brazilian state will not be reformed from within. [? if pcdo_in_coalition == 1: Our continued participation in the coalition becomes increasingly difficult to justify on these terms. ?]

The repression, when it comes, will be severe. We must be ready.

@support_reformist
subtitle: Push for a limited, achievable program within the constitutional order.
unavailable-subtitle: The reformist tendency is not strong enough.
choose-if: historical_mode == 0 or (reformist_strength >= structural_strength and reformist_strength >= moderate_strength and reformist_strength >= combative_strength)
on-arrival: limited_plan_support += 1; reformist_strength += 8; reformist_dissent -= 5; combative_dissent += 6; structural_dissent += 5
go-to: limited_adopted if limited_plan_support >= 2; limited_progress if limited_plan_support < 2

@limited_progress

We advocate for a realistic, immediate program: price controls on staple goods, a modest land statute extending tenancy protections to rural workers, wage indexation to inflation, and profit remittance controls on foreign companies. These measures stop well short of structural transformation, but they are achievable, defensible to the middle class, and less likely to provoke a violent reaction.

The combative tendency accuses us of retreating into reformism in the face of a genuinely revolutionary situation. We have not yet adopted the plan formally. More work needs to be done.

@limited_adopted
on-arrival: reformist_strength += 10; reformist_dissent -= 10; combative_dissent += 8; structural_dissent += 6; limited_reforms_adopted = 1; reformas_de_base = 2; coup_progress -= 1 if coup_progress > 0; workers_pcdo += 3*(1-dissent)

With sustained effort, we have convinced the party to adopt the limited reform program. [? if pcdo_in_coalition == 1: We can begin pressing for these measures within the government immediately — they are modest enough that even some conservative ministers may support parts of them. ?]

It is not the revolution. But it may keep the door open long enough to build the forces we need.

@defer

We hold off on committing to a line, awaiting greater clarity on the political situation. The combative tendency is withering in its contempt — in a revolutionary situation, they say, indecision is itself a political choice. But caution has its own logic when the stakes are this high.